# =============================================================================
# Files an AI coding assistant must not read while working on Forge.
#
# Two different kinds of rule live below, and the difference matters:
#
#   1. LEAK DEFENCE - credentials and private data. One of these reaching a
#      model is an incident, not an inconvenience. Assume anything read is
#      retained somewhere you do not control.
#   2. NOISE - generated, vendored, or enormous files. Excluding them costs
#      nothing and keeps the assistant reading code instead of lockfiles.
#      (.venv alone is 6,500+ files.)
#
# Keep this file, .perplexityignore, and .gitignore in step. A path that is
# git-ignored but not listed here is still on disk, and still readable.
#
# IMPORTANT: an ignore file is a hint to a tool, not an access control. It
# does not stop a shell command from reading the file. The enforced equivalent
# for Claude Code is a `permissions.deny` rule in .claude/settings.json.
# =============================================================================

# --- Credentials: never, under any circumstances --------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.keystore
*.jks
id_rsa
id_dsa
id_ecdsa
id_ed25519
*.ppk
.netrc
_netrc
.npmrc
.pypirc
secrets/
secret/
credentials
credentials.*
*.credentials
*.token
*_token
*.secret

# --- Cloud and CI credentials that end up in a working tree by accident ---
.aws/
.azure/
.gcloud/
gha-creds-*.json
service-account*.json
*-service-account.json

# --- Local agent, editor, and tool state ----------------------------------
# These can hold prompts, file contents, absolute paths, and occasionally a
# key that was pasted into a chat.
.claude/settings.local.json
.forge/local/
.aider*
.cursor/
.vscode/settings.json
.idea/

# --- Python: generated, vendored, or huge ---------------------------------
.venv/
venv/
env/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
build/
dist/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.tox/
.nox/
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/

# --- Generated lockfile ----------------------------------------------------
# 855 lines of pinned versions and hashes, with no design signal in it.
# pyproject.toml is the file that answers "what does this depend on".
uv.lock

# --- Version control internals --------------------------------------------
.git/

# --- Data and logs ---------------------------------------------------------
# Forge has no data directory today. The rules are here so that adding one
# does not quietly open a hole: a log or a local database can contain
# anything a user ever fed to the tool.
data/
*.log
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.db
*.dump
*.bak

# --- Binaries and archives -------------------------------------------------
*.whl
*.tar.gz
*.zip
*.so
*.dll
*.dylib
*.exe

# --- Not used by Forge today; listed so that adding them is already covered
node_modules/
.next/
coverage/

# --- Deliberately NOT ignored ---------------------------------------------
# examples/    - small, deliberately flawed sample files. They exist to be
#                read, and contain no secrets by rule (see examples/README.md).
# .env.example - documents every supported key with empty values. It is the
#                thing a contributor is supposed to read.
